-- title:
--   Frobenius and uniformiser for an unramified level over L^S
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ and write $\mathbb{Q}_q \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}_q}$ for the $q$-adic field and an algebraic closure, with norms taken in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}_q}$. Let $L$ be an intermediate field, finite over $\mathbb{Q}_q$, and let $G$ be a finite group acting faithfully on $L$ by semiring automorphisms that fix every element of $\mathbb{Q}_q$ (hypothesis `hG`), together with an action on the unit group $L^\times$ compatible with the action on $L$ (hypothesis `hcompat`). Let $S \le G$ be a subgroup and $K'$ an intermediate field, finite over $\mathbb{Q}_q$, with $K' \le L$ and such that an element of $L$ lies in $K'$ exactly when it is fixed by every $s \in S$; thus $K' = L^{S}$. Let $N \trianglelefteq G$ be normal, and let $\pi \in L^\times$ be fixed by all of $G$, satisfy $\|\pi\| < 1$, and be of maximal norm among $N$-fixed elements of $L$ of norm $< 1$. The conclusion asserts the existence of $\psi \in S$ and $\pi' \in L^\times$ such that: every element of the quotient of $S$ by $N \cap S$ (formed as $N$ restricted to $S$) lies in the cyclic subgroup generated by the class of $\psi$; for every $x \in L$ fixed by $N \sqcap S$ with $\|x\| \le 1$ one has $\|\psi \cdot x - x^{\,\#k}\| < 1$, where $\#k$ is the cardinality of the residue field of the valuation ring of $K'$ obtained by pulling back the unit ball of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}_q}$ along $K' \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}_q}$; and $\pi'$ is fixed by every $s \in S$, satisfies $\|\pi'\| < 1$, and has maximal norm among elements of $L$ fixed by $N \sqcap S$ of norm $< 1$.
--
--   This is the statement that a level $N$ which is unramified over the base $L^{G}$, witnessed by the $G$-invariant uniformiser $\pi$, remains unramified over the sub-base $K' = L^{S}$: the group $S/(N \cap S)$ is cyclic, generated by an element acting on integral $(N \cap S)$-fixed elements as the $\#k_{K'}$-power map modulo elements of norm $< 1$, and an $S$-invariant element $\pi'$ of maximal norm $< 1$ plays the role of a uniformiser at that level. It supplies the sub-base hypotheses used by [`ExtCitation.LocalLevel.isLocalFundamentalClass_map_subtype`](thm.html#ExtCitation.LocalLevel.isLocalFundamentalClass_map_subtype) and by [`ExtCitation.LocalLevel.range_infNatTrans_eq_of_unramified_level`](thm.html#ExtCitation.LocalLevel.range_infNatTrans_eq_of_unramified_level).
